{"id": "fu-01", "trap": false, "gap_notes": "Reflexion limits long-term memory to a sliding window and suggests vector or SQL memory stores; it can settle in local minima, and test-driven code feedback is unreliable for non-deterministic or impure functions. ExpeL is limited to text observations and closed-source API models, and its insights must fit in the context window, so lifelong learning would need retrieval over insights."}
{"id": "fu-02", "trap": false, "gap_notes": "Models struggle to use information in the middle of long contexts, and reader performance saturates well before retriever recall; the role of architecture, query-aware contextualization and instruction tuning is only studied preliminarily. Robustness to irrelevant retrieved context is shown in-domain, with a general robust retrieval-augmented model left open and the NLI filter discarding relevant passages."}
